<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#weser-renaissance">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Weser Renaissance</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> is the style of Renaissance architecture in the Weser region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, flourishing from about 1520 to 1620. Drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Flemish models, it is marked by richly decorated gables, bay windows and bands of carved ornament. Notable examples include the facade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> town hall, added in 1608–1612, and the Hochzeitshaus in Hamelin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/weser-renaissance.jpg" alt="Weser Renaissance" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Times · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juleum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Between the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> region saw a building boom. The river carried trade and ideas and marked the north–south extent of a cultural region that reached west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a> and east as far as Wolfsburg. Castles, manor houses, town halls, houses and churches of the period survive in unusually high density, because the region's economy recovered only slowly from the Thirty Years War and there was no money for the baroque rebuilding seen in parts of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the 16th century the nobility turned medieval castles into residences. Early ones often had two wings; later the favoured plan was an enclosed courtyard whose wings met at corner towers holding staircases, a form the lesser nobility soon copied. The Zwerchhaus, a cross-gable with so-called welsch or Italian gables, served as a sign of power on castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detmold">Detmold</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celle">Celle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buckeburg">Bückeburg</a>, where it could be seen from far beyond the ramparts.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">Burghers</a> copied these forms to show their growing influence. Town halls such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celle">Celle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemgo">Lemgo</a> received gables along their sides, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>'s a whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> facade. Large townhouses with a great gateway into an inner hall rose from Nienburg to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a>, Hamelin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoxter">Höxter</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hann-munden">Hannoversch Münden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einbeck">Einbeck</a>. Other marks of the style are bossage stone, projecting bays called Utluchten and double windows. In churches the pulpit was raised and set facing the pews, as in the castle chapels of Celle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buckeburg">Bückeburg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The term was coined around 1912 by Richard Klapheck, and Max Sonnen used it in 1918 in his book Die Weserrenaissance, classing buildings on purely formal grounds. The idea of an independent regional style grew out of the nationalistic thinking of the late 19th century. In 1964 Jürgen Soenke and the photographer Herbert Kreft published an inventory under the same title that called the style folk art; it ran to six editions by 1986 and made the term widely known.</p><p>Hitchcock gave the term international standing in his German Renaissance Architecture of 1981, while stressing its links with Renaissance architecture as a whole. Research by the Weser Renaissance Museum at Brake Castle, founded in 1986, has since criticised the notion of a regional identity, pointing instead to architectural drawings, architects from elsewhere, builders working across regions and the Europe-wide demands of court fashion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Weser Renaissance” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Gothic and <b>Weser Renaissance</b> town hall and the Roland statue were listed together as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2004, and the Brothers Grimm's tale of the Town Musicians of Bremen made the city's name familiar to children worldwide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
